\name{InterMetaAIReport}
\alias{InterMetaAIProvider}
\alias{InterMetaAIInterpret}
\alias{InterMetaAIReport}
\title{Create Citation-Constrained AI Interpretations and Reports}
\usage{
InterMetaAIProvider(generate, name = "custom", model = "custom")

InterMetaAIInterpret(
  context,
  evidence,
  provider,
  language = c("en", "zh"),
  max_records_per_pathway = 30L,
  purpose = c("biology_exploration", "prognostic_validation")
)

InterMetaAIReport(
  context,
  evidence = NULL,
  interpretation = NULL,
  provider = NULL,
  language = c("en", "zh"),
  outdir = NULL,
  max_records_per_pathway = 30L,
  purpose = NULL
)
}
\arguments{
  \item{generate}{A function with arguments \code{messages} and \code{schema}.
  It must call the user's chosen model service and return a parsed R list or
  data frame matching the supplied schema. Secrets must be resolved by this
  function at invocation time, not stored in the provider object.}
  \item{name}{Non-secret provider label.}
  \item{model}{Non-secret model label.}
  \item{context}{An object produced by \code{InterMetaAIContext()}.}
  \item{evidence}{Optional object produced by
  \code{InterMetaPubMedSearch()}.}
  \item{provider}{Optional provider created by
  \code{InterMetaAIProvider()}, \code{InterMetaLLMProvider()}, or
  \code{InterMetaDeepSeekProvider()}.}
  \item{interpretation}{Optional previously generated interpretation. It is
  revalidated against the supplied context and evidence.}
  \item{language}{Language for human-readable interpretation text.}
  \item{max_records_per_pathway}{Positive integer maximum number of unique
  PubMed records sent to the model for each selected pathway. Records are
  deduplicated by pathway-specific PMID. Exploration uses round-robin retrieval
  layers and lexical study-type hints to diversify the selection; the hints do
  not establish study classification, quality or guaranteed coverage. Original
  retrieval order is preserved within each hint queue. Selection details and
  limitations are retained in provenance.}
  \item{purpose}{Interpretation goal. Fresh calls default to
  \code{biology_exploration}, which integrates functional, omics and patient
  evidence without requiring a clinical endpoint match. Use
  \code{prognostic_validation} for the existing strict endpoint rules.
  For \code{InterMetaAIReport()}, \code{NULL} preserves the supplied
  interpretation's purpose; legacy interpretations without purpose retain
  \code{prognostic_validation}. The goal cannot be silently changed when reusing
  a provider-visible interpretation.}
  \item{outdir}{Optional new output directory. Existing paths are never
  overwritten.}
}
\value{
\code{InterMetaAIProvider()} returns an \code{InterMetaAIProvider} adapter.
\code{InterMetaAIInterpret()} returns an
\code{InterMetaAIInterpretation}. \code{InterMetaAIReport()} returns an
\code{InterMetaAIReport}; when \code{outdir} is supplied it also publishes a
complete report directory. Schema 4 adds \code{exploration_summary}, one row
per selected pathway, and \code{exploration_findings}, with new calls requesting
at most five non-redundant source-linked findings per pathway. Distinct findings
may share a publication where needed. In \code{biology_exploration}, these two
exploration tables are authoritative. Existing \code{claims},
\code{assessments}, \code{pathway_synthesis}, and \code{biology_findings}
outputs are retained only for legacy prognostic compatibility and are marked
\code{not_applicable}; their placeholders do not indicate model failure or
insufficient evidence. Schema 4.3.0 records this table-role metadata while
retaining all major-version-4 figure inputs. Schema 4.2.0 added
finding-level \code{Quote.Verified}, \code{Quote.Match.Method},
\code{Disease.Match.Verified}, and \code{Eligible.For.Summary}, plus pathway-level \code{Quote.Grounding} and
\code{Evidence.Eligibility}.
}
\description{
Constrains model output to selected pathways and PubMed identifiers present
in the actual supplied model input. Fresh reports explore tumour biology;
endpoint-specific prognostic validation remains an explicit alternative.
Fixed non-causal and non-clinical notices accompany both modes.
}
\details{
Report schema 4.2.0 makes biological exploration the fresh-call default and
retains the 16-file report bundle; reports previously generated by version
0.10.0 remain schema 4.0.0. Provenance records the provider-visible AI contract
revision \code{20260905-quote-replay-v1} and the independent validation revision
\code{20260906-quote-quarantine-v1}. The provider-visible revision is unchanged,
so a compatible parsed candidate created by the earlier contract can be
revalidated without another provider call. The separate validation revision
allows a server entry to reject stale installed or in-memory 1.0.0 code.
Each pathway response contains \code{exploration}, with
\code{biological_summary} (at most 420 characters), \code{context_dependence},
\code{research_hypothesis}, and \code{findings}. Each finding contains a
pathway-unique \code{finding_id}, \code{pmid}, \code{study_disease},
\code{disease_match}, \code{scope}, \code{evidence_types},
\code{biological_processes}, \code{observation}, \code{effect},
\code{study_context}, \code{abstract_quote}, and \code{cohort_id}.
The last field identifies a public cohort, or \code{unknown}, never a sample
or patient. Multiple findings may share a PMID. Exploration findings do not
require clinical \code{claims} or \code{assessments}.

Evidence types include cell, animal and organoid experiments; bioinformatics
and omics; human tissue, clinical observation and clinical intervention;
reviews and other evidence. The nonexclusive functional, omics and patient
channels list unique PMIDs, not evidence quality or independent replications.
Reviews have their own channel and cannot simultaneously be labelled as a
primary experiment. Component evidence contributes without being relabelled
whole-pathway evidence. Findings with opposing, null or context-dependent
observations are retained. Abstract-excerpt checks establish lexical source
grounding. Target-disease eligibility checks consistency of the model-supplied
\code{Study.Disease} and \code{Disease.Match} fields with the context target
after alias normalization; it does not independently infer the paper's disease
from the Abstract or MeSH. Neither operation establishes semantic truth. Biological processes distinguish angiogenesis,
stromal and immune microenvironmental effects. The \code{effect} field describes
the response to the finding's stated exposure or intervention, never a universal
up/down direction assigned to an entire pathway. A qualifying non-empty
exploration quote must be one contiguous verbatim Abstract excerpt with at least
20 Unicode letter-or-number characters. Matching first collapses whitespace
and then, only if needed, applies an explicit set of Unicode presentation
equivalents for spaces, quotation marks, typographic dashes, soft hyphens and
ellipsis glyphs. The derived \code{Quote.Match.Method} records
\code{whitespace_exact}, \code{typography_equivalent}, \code{empty},
\code{insufficient_information}, \code{overlength}, or
\code{source_mismatch}. A non-empty quote that is too short uses
\code{insufficient_information}; one longer than 1,000 characters uses
\code{overlength}; one that is not a contiguous source excerpt uses
\code{source_mismatch}. These rows are quarantined individually rather than
causing an otherwise auditable report to fail. They are not rewritten or fuzzily
accepted. Their original model text is retained with
\code{Quote.Verified = FALSE} and \code{Eligible.For.Summary = FALSE}, and they
do not contribute to finding-derived evidence channels, PMID or process
aggregates, or source-linked observations. Case, words, accents, Greek letters,
mathematical symbols and ordering are not fuzzy-matched.
The unchanged provider-visible request contract still asks for at least eight
characters for compatibility with the saved trial05 request. The independent
local validator deliberately applies the stricter 20-character rule above.

The pathway-level \code{Quote.Grounding} field is \code{complete} when all
finding rows have verified quotes, \code{partial} when only some do,
\code{none} when findings exist but none has a verified quote, and
\code{no_findings} when there are no finding rows. Quote grounding is distinct
from the structured target-disease label-consistency check; both must pass for
a row to be eligible for the derived pathway evidence summary. The latter is
exposed as \code{Disease.Match.Verified} in the finding table.

The pathway-level \code{Evidence.Eligibility} field is \code{complete},
\code{partial}, \code{none}, or \code{no_findings}, derived from eligible/all
findings after both strict quote verification and the target-disease
label-consistency check.
It must not be inferred from \code{Quote.Grounding}, which deliberately measures
quote matching only. When eligibility is partial, the public
\code{Biological.Summary} is constructed only from eligible
\code{Source.Linked.Observations}; public \code{Context.Dependence} and
\code{Research.Hypothesis} are suppressed. The original
\code{Model.Biological.Summary}, \code{Model.Context.Dependence}, and
\code{Model.Research.Hypothesis} remain audit-only and are not silently presented
as the grounded synthesis.

Exploration status distinguishes \code{provided}, \code{no_relevant_findings},
\code{ungrounded_findings}, \code{not_assessed}, and \code{input_failed}.
No counted PMIDs means none qualified in the supplied selection, not proof
that no relevant literature exists. The clinical endpoint remains statistical
context and does not gate biological relevance. The signed network score is
not a measurement of pathway activity, flux, causal importance or clinical utility.

Exploration keeps abstracts up to 4000 characters each when their actual
combined capped length fits 200000 characters. Otherwise a common cap is
water-filled so short abstracts retain their full text. Exact reading bounds,
selection hints and provider-visible fingerprints are recorded. Legacy
interpretations are revalidated with their original selection algorithm.

In \code{prognostic_validation} mode, schema 3's \code{biology_synthesis}
remains in each pathway response.
It contains \code{mechanism_summary}, \code{limitations}, and a \code{findings}
array with \code{pmid}, \code{study_disease}, \code{disease_match}, \code{scope},
\code{finding_type}, \code{study_design}, \code{relation}, \code{claim}, and
\code{abstract_quote}. Findings distinguish whole-pathway from component
evidence, mechanisms from associations/background, and supporting from opposing,
null, or unclear findings. Every biological PMID must also be cited and clinically
assessed for the same pathway. Source excerpts must be in the actual model input.

The biological labels are \code{pathway_mechanism}, \code{indirect_context},
\code{conflicting_evidence}, \code{insufficient_evidence}, and \code{not_assessed}.
Only same-disease experimental pathway-level mechanisms can receive the first
label. A gene signature, isolated metabolite, review, or cited mention is not
automatically upgraded. These are qualitative evidence descriptions, not
validated confidence scores or proof that a pathway is biologically correct.
The signed network score is not a measurement of pathway activity or flux.
Legacy interpretations without biological synthesis remain \code{not_assessed}.
Model mechanism narratives are retained as explicitly unverified audit material;
human review must check semantic support, contradictions, scope, and study overlap.
Clinical null associations do not suppress independent experimental mechanisms;
neutral biological findings are identified within the biological assessment.
Revalidation checks context and provider-visible evidence fingerprints as well as
stored selection fields. These detect inconsistent reuse, not authenticate an
untrusted party's editable report. Legacy missing fingerprints remain disclosed.

Clinical rules introduced in schema 2 remain independent and unchanged.
Each pathway response includes an \code{assessments}
array, one item per cited PMID, containing \code{pmid}, \code{evidence_type},
\code{study_endpoint}, \code{endpoint_match}, \code{direction},
\code{directness}, and \code{abstract_quote}. Study types are
\code{human_prognosis}, \code{human_incidence}, \code{human_tissue},
\code{preclinical}, \code{review}, \code{other}, and \code{unknown}.
Quotes are checked against the supplied abstract after whitespace normalization.
This grounding check does not validate the semantic classification itself.

Allowed literature-consistency values are \code{supported}, \code{mixed},
\code{potentially_contradicted}, and \code{insufficient_evidence}. These labels
are derived only from direct human prognostic evidence with a matching target
endpoint, a verified abstract quote, and an explicitly same or opposite
direction. Both directions produce \code{mixed}. Incidence results,
preclinical mechanisms, indirect pathway evidence, and unmatched endpoints are
ineligible. A null association is never counted as an opposite direction.
Unknown endpoints and legacy outputs lacking assessments are conservatively
insufficient. Raw model labels and narratives are retained separately for audit;
they are not endorsed conclusions. The final labels describe the cited subset
of retrieved literature; they do not validate
biological truth or establish causality. A model response that invents a
pathway, uses another status, or cites a PMID absent from that pathway's records
sent to the model is rejected. Every claim needs a citation. The three
non-insufficient states require a cited claim; a pathway without records must
use \code{insufficient_evidence}. These checks establish source membership,
not semantic support for every assertion, so human review remains necessary.
Citation identifiers and URLs must occur only in the structured citation
fields, not in summary or claim text. Uncited \code{insufficient_evidence}
summaries are replaced with a fixed evidence-insufficiency statement.

No model is contacted unless a provider is explicitly supplied. Without a
provider, \code{InterMetaAIReport()} still creates a deterministic evidence
skeleton. The provider receives aggregate context only; expression matrices,
survival rows, and sample identifiers are excluded by the context and report
layers. Model inputs use field whitelists. Each abstract is limited to 4,000
characters and the combined abstract budget is 200,000 characters. Exploration
uses the water-filled allocation described above; prognostic and legacy
selection divides the budget evenly across records. Interaction and driver-pair tables are limited
to 200 rows each; concatenated \code{Shared.MGP} strings are omitted from model
input because individual driver pairs are supplied separately. All truncation
is disclosed in provenance and warnings.

When provided by a built-in client, allowlisted non-sensitive call metadata
is retained as \code{provenance$provider_call}, including model identifiers,
usage, settings, request/response hashes, and, for an offline candidate replay,
the replay flag plus candidate SHA-256, format version, and validation revision.
Raw responses, credentials,
reasoning text, and arbitrary metadata fields are not retained.

If \code{outdir} is used, publication is staged and then renamed as a complete
directory. It contains \code{report.md}, \code{report.rds},
\code{evidence.tsv}, \code{claims.tsv}, \code{assessments.tsv},
\code{pathway_synthesis.tsv}, \code{biology_findings.tsv},
\code{exploration_summary.tsv}, \code{exploration_findings.tsv}, \code{queries.tsv},
\code{searches.tsv}, \code{response_hashes.tsv}, \code{warnings.txt},
\code{provenance.rds}, a SHA-256 manifest, and a completion marker. The function
refuses to replace an existing directory.

For biology exploration, \code{report.md}, \code{report.rds}, and provenance
record the authoritative-versus-compatibility table semantics. TSV attributes
are not relied upon for this distinction.
}
\examples{
data("InterMetaExampleScore")
context <- InterMetaAIContext(
  score = InterMetaExampleScore,
  disease = "example cancer",
  endpoint = "overall survival",
  direction = "unfavorable"
)

# A useful report skeleton is available without network or model calls.
report <- InterMetaAIReport(context, language = "en")
report$top_pathways

# Provider adapters are intentionally vendor-neutral. A real adapter reads its
# credentials at call time and returns parsed data matching `schema`.
provider <- InterMetaAIProvider(
  generate = function(messages, schema) {
    list(pathways = list())
  },
  name = "example",
  model = "example"
)
}
